## 4. The skeptical discipline (Markowsky-style)

| Claim | Status | Why it fails / holds |
|---|---|---|
| **Parthenon designed on φ** | **Fails** | Markowsky, "Misconceptions about the Golden Ratio," *College Mathematics Journal* 23 (1992): measurements of the façade do not fit golden rectangles within tolerance; no Greek text prescribes φ for architecture; Euclid post-dates the building. Documented canon = **4:9 integer ratios** (Penrose 1888, Korres 1995). |
| **Great Pyramid encodes φ** (apothem/half-base ≈ φ) | **Fails as intent; survives as arithmetic accident** | seked 5½ → apothem/half-base = √(7²+5.5²)/5.5 = 1.6186. Any seked-5½ pyramid does this. Herodotus II.124 does not say what pyramidologists claim (Smyth's reading; debunked by Petrie 1883). Rossi 2004: Egyptian mathematics used rational slopes; no φ anywhere in the corpus. |
| **Great Pyramid encodes π** (half-perimeter/height = 2π) | **Same verdict** | 2×440/280 = 22/7 = 3.1429 — the well-known Egyptian *approximation* is indeed 22/7-family arithmetic (RMP problem 48 uses 64/81 ≈ 3.16 for area), but the pyramid ratio is a **by-product of seked 5½**, not an encoding. |
| **Vesica piscis governs all of Chartres** | **Partly fails** | Vesica (√3) appears in bay proportions (plausible, documented in lodge practice); whole-building vesica schemes in popular literature (e.g., Lawlor, *Sacred Geometry*, 1982) are uncontrolled curve-fitting — with two adjustable points, √3 can be "found" anywhere. |
| **Angkor Wat solar alignment** | **Survives scrutiny** | Published in *Science* (1976) with measured azimuths; repeatable. Distinguish from Mannikka's numerological cubit-reading thesis, parts of which remain contested. |
| **Angkor/Teotihuacan φ or "hunab" units** | **Fails** | No epigraphic support; unit-of-measure claims (Harleston) not reproducible by independent surveys. |
| **El Castillo calendrical counts** | **Holds (counts); intentionality of serpent shadow debated** | 4×91+1, 18, 52 are verifiable on-site counts stand